+---
+name: verify-deliverables
+description: Final quality pass before reporting completion - manifest check, reopen every produced file, count reconciliation against sources, prohibited-term sweep, cross-file consistency. Load once all requested output files exist.
+---
+
+# Verify deliverables
+
+Run these checks in order, using Bash and the read tool. Fix anything that fails, then re-check only the files you changed. Do not skip to the final message until every check passes or the failure is explained in your final message.
+
+1. Manifest. `ls -l <output dir>`. Every deliverable the request named must exist under its exact filename (spelling, version suffix, extension). A missing or misnamed file is a failure - produce it before continuing.
+2. Reopen. Read each produced file start to end with the read tool. Confirm: non-empty, section headings the request asked for are present, no truncation, no leftover placeholder or template text, tables and code blocks intact.
+3. Count reconciliation. For each structured source, compute its item count programmatically - for delimited files run `python3 scripts/normalize_delimited.py <source file>` (it prints ROW_COUNT and per-column fill counts; add `--inner ';'` if cells contain inner-delimited values) - and compare against the number of items the deliverables actually document and any counts stated in prose. Publish only numbers that reconcile; correct the prose if it disagrees.
+4. Prohibited-content sweep. From the request, collect the forbidden terms, internal-only markers and known-wrong names (things that must not be documented). `grep -rni` each across the output directory. Every hit must be an explicit negation or warning, otherwise remove or reword it.
+5. Cross-file consistency. For facts that appear in more than one deliverable (names, versions, limits, dates, paths, endpoint lists), grep all occurrences and confirm every occurrence agrees. Resolve conflicts against the authoritative source, then fix every file that repeated the wrong value.
+6. Self-report. One line per check: pass or fail. If any check failed and was fixed, say what changed.
